  "worked_solution": "We're given that there exists a point on the line where $z=0.$ Substituting this to the first equation gives\n\n$$\n2x-0=0\\,\u27f9\\,x=0,\n$$\n\nand substituting $z=0$ into the second equation gives\n\n$$\n2y-0-6=0\\,\u27f9\\,y=3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a=\u27e80,3,0\u27e9$ is a position vector of a point on the line.\n\nNext, we notice:\n\n- For the plane $2x-3z=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=\u27e82,0,-3\u27e9.$\n\n- For the plane $2y-3z-6=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=\u27e80,2,-3\u27e9.$\n\nTo find a direction vector for the line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}b & ={n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} \\\\  & =\u27e82,0,-3\u27e9\\times \u27e80,2,-3\u27e9 \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 2 & 0 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -3\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =6i+6j+4k \\\\  & =\u27e86,6,4\u27e9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the equation of the line is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+tb \\\\ r(t) & =\u27e80,3,0\u27e9+t\u27e86,6,4\u27e9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally then, ${a}_{1}+{a}_{2}=0+3=3,$ and $b=\u27e86,6,4\u27e9.$",
